  {
   "_id": 5179,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">The modulo operator (%) plays a crucial role in maintaining the circular structure of a circular queue implemented using arrays. It ensures that the rear and front pointers wrap around to the beginning of the array when they reach the end, thereby allowing continuous use of memory without overflow (as long as space is available).</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Understanding the Problem Without Modulo:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">In a <b>linear queue</b>, when the rear reaches the end of the array (i.e., rear == size - 1), you cannot insert more elements even if there are empty slots at the beginning (due to dequeued elements). This leads to <b>memory wastage</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Purpose of Modulo in Circular Queue:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">To solve this, we use the <b>modulo operator (%)</b> with the array size to <b>wrap around</b> the rear and front pointers.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Key Operations Using Modulo:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Enqueue (Insert Element):</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">rear = (rear + 1) % size;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">This ensures that after reaching the last index (size - 1), the rear moves back to index 0 if space is available.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Dequeue (Remove Element):</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">front = (front + 1) % size;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Similarly, the front pointer wraps around to the beginning.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Why is Modulo Important?</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Maintains Circular Nature:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">It allows the queue to behave like a circle by reusing emptied positions at the beginning of the array.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Prevents Array Index Out of Bounds:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Without modulo, incrementing rear or front beyond the array size would cause illegal memory access or overflow.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Simplifies Logic:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">No need to manually reset pointers to zero when they reach the end; modulo handles it automatically.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Efficient Queue Operations:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-left:8px; text-align:justify\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Modulo helps maintain O(1) time complexity by avoiding element shifting or dynamic memory allocation.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ol>\n",
