   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Aptos&quot;,sans-serif\">Why is the modulo (%) operator essential in implementing circular queue operations using arrays? Explain its role in maintaining the circular nature of the queue.</span></span></span></b></p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 311,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 5179
  }
 ],
 "lq": [
  {
   "_id": 5198,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>1. Justify the Use of a Circular Queue for Round-Robin Scheduling:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">A circular queue is ideal for managing player turns in a round-robin game because:</span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Circular Nature Matches Game Logic: In round-robin scheduling, after the last player finishes, the turn should return to the first player. This directly aligns with how circular queues operate, where the rear wraps back to the beginning.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Efficient Use of Memory: Unlike linear queues, which cannot reuse freed-up spaces at the front after dequeue operations, a circular queue reuses memory efficiently by wrapping around. This is essential in games with limited memory usage (e.g., mobile games).</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Consistent Turn Flow: Players are enqueued once and the game keeps rotating their turns by dequeuing the front player and enqueuing them again at the rear (if they remain in the game). This maintains fairness and order.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Avoids Shifting: No need to shift elements after each turn, making it more efficient in terms of time complexity (O(1) for both enqueue and dequeue).</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>2. Pseudocode for Enqueue Operation in Circular Queue</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">#define SIZE 100</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">int queue[SIZE];</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">int front = -1, rear = -1;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">void enqueue(int playerID) {</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; // Check if queue is full</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; if ((rear + 1) % SIZE == front) {</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; printf(&quot;Queue is Full. Cannot add new player.\\n&quot;);</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; return;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; }</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; // First element insertion</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; if (front == -1 &amp;&amp; rear == -1) {</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; front = rear = 0;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; queue[rear] = playerID;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; } else {</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; rear = (rear + 1) % SIZE;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; queue[rear] = playerID;</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; }</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; printf(&quot;Player %d added to the turn queue.\\n&quot;, playerID);</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">}</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>3. What Happens When Rear Reaches the End of the Array?</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">In a circular queue:</span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">The rear index keeps moving forward as new players are added.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">When rear reaches the <b>end of the array (index SIZE - 1)</b> and another player needs to be added, instead of producing an overflow, the rear is <b>wrapped around to the beginning</b> of the array (index 0) <b>using modulo</b>:</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; rear = (rear + 1) % SIZE</span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">This wrapping ensures that <b>previously freed-up space at the front of the queue</b> (from dequeued players) can now be <b>reused</b>.</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Example:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Let&rsquo;s say SIZE = 5. Queue starts empty.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">1.Players 101, 102, 103, 104 are added.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">rear = 3, front = 0</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">queue = [101, 102, 103, 104, _]</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">2.One player is dequeued (101), so front moves to index 1.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">queue = [_, 102, 103, 104, _]</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">3. A new player (105) is added.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"list-style-type:none\">\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">rear = (3 + 1) % 5 = 4</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">queue = [_, 102, 103, 104, 105]</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">4. Another player (106) is added.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"list-style-type:none\">\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">rear = (4 + 1) % 5 = 0 &rarr; wrap-around!</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">queue = [106, 102, 103, 104, 105]</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Thus, space is reused, and the queue behaves circularly.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Circular queues are perfectly suited for round-robin scenarios like player turn management in games. They provide efficient, fair, and memory-optimized scheduling. The modulo logic ensures that the queue wraps around without requiring extra space or data shifting, making gameplay smoother and system-friendly.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n",
